"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT2c, negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. The preoperative PSA was 19. The biopsy findings showed a Gleason 3+4 pattern, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen grade of ISUP grade group 3. These favorable pathological findings, including negative margins and no nodal involvement, are positive prognostic factors. Conversely, the high preoperative PSA of 19 represents a significant risk factor. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. The current prognosis is informed by the surgical findings, but postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes are critical variables that would revise this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 61.10 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.83–76.38 months; its coverage has not been validated."
